There is no need to keep the whole table when only the last two results are used. `oneBack` stores the best result up to the previous house, while `twoBack` stores the result up to the house before that.

For each value, taking the house means adding it to `twoBack`. Skipping it means keeping `oneBack`. After calculating the new result, the two stored values move forward.

```java
class Solution {
    public int houseRobber(int[] money) {
        int twoBack = 0;
        int oneBack = 0;

        for (int currentMoney : money) {
            int take = twoBack + currentMoney;
            int skip = oneBack;
            int currentBest = Math.max(take, skip);

            twoBack = oneBack;
            oneBack = currentBest;
        }

        return oneBack;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, using only a few integer variables. No values are changed in the input array.

The recursive formula is fine, but the same index can be reached from different paths. This version stores the answer for each starting index in `memo`, so each index is calculated only once.

An empty list returns `0`, and the base case beyond the last house also returns `0`. That keeps the recursion simple for one house and for no houses.

```java
class Solution {
    private int findBest(int[] money, int index, int[] memo) {
        if (index >= money.length) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[index] != -1) {
            return memo[index];
        }

        int skip = findBest(money, index + 1, memo);
        int take = money[index] + findBest(money, index + 2, memo);
        memo[index] = Math.max(skip, take);
        return memo[index];
    }

    public int houseRobber(int[] money) {
        int[] memo = new int[money.length];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(memo, -1);
        return findBest(money, 0, memo);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` since each index is solved once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo array and the recursion stack. The input array remains unchanged.

A direct recursive version makes the two choices at every house: skip it and move to the next one, or take its money and move two positions ahead. The larger result is returned.

This is useful for seeing the recurrence, but it repeats the same calls many times. For example, with `[2, 7, 9, 3, 1]`, the best choice is `2 + 9 + 1 = 12`.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    int findBest(const vector<int>& money, int index) {
        if (index >= static_cast<int>(money.size())) {
            return 0;
        }

        int skip = findBest(money, index + 1);
        int take = money[index] + findBest(money, index + 2);
        return max(skip, take);
    }

public:
    int houseRobber(vector<int>& money) {
        return findBest(money, 0);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` because both choices are explored repeatedly.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack. The input array is not changed.

This version fills the answers from right to left. `best[i]` is the most money possible starting at house `i`. At each position, the choices are to skip the house or take it and continue from `i + 2`.

The extra two zero entries represent positions after the last house. They make the boundary checks easy and also cover an empty vector.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int houseRobber(vector<int>& money) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(money.size());
        vector<int> best(n + 2, 0);

        for (int index = n - 1; index >= 0; --index) {
            int skip = best[index + 1];
            int take = money[index] + best[index + 2];
            best[index] = max(skip, take);
        }

        return best[0];
    }
};
```

For `[1, 2, 3, 1]`, the best values are built backward and the first entry becomes `4`, from taking the first and third houses.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the reverse DP array. The input vector is not changed.

